    <h1>How to buy research peptides from China</h1>
    <p class="lead muted">A practical sequence for first-time and repeat buyers. The goal is not the lowest quote — it is a shipment you can defend with documents.</p>

    <h2>1. Qualify the supplier before the product</h2>
    <p>Ask for the business licence, the bank account name and the invoice entity. They must match. A supplier that cannot align these three is a reseller with no control over production.</p>

    <h2>2. Send a structured RFQ</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>SKU list with strengths and quantities</li>
      <li>Destination country and port/city</li>
      <li>Documentation requirements (COA, MSDS, certificate of origin)</li>
      <li>Packaging and labelling requirements</li>
      <li>Target delivery window</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A vague "price list please" gets a vague answer. A structured RFQ gets a quotable one.</p>

    <h2>3. Ask for the batch COA before payment</h2>
    <p>The COA must be for the lot you will receive, with HPLC chromatogram and MS spectrum. If the supplier sends a generic product COA, treat it as a warning.</p>

    <h2>4. Sample before volume</h2>
    <p>For a new SKU or a new supplier, order a sample lot and test it in your own workflow. The sample's COA batch number must match the shipment.</p>

    <h2>5. Agree Incoterm and payment</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Who arranges freight</th><th>Risk transfer</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>EXW</td><td>Buyer</td><td>At supplier premises</td></tr>
        <tr><td>FOB</td><td>Buyer (after loading)</td><td>On board</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CIF</td><td>Supplier</td><td>At destination port</td></tr>
        <tr><td>DDP</td><td>Supplier</td><td>At buyer's address</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>For first orders, T/T against documents is common. Credit terms come after a track record.</p>

    <h2>6. Prepare for customs</h2>
    <p>Confirm the commodity description, HS code and any import permit requirements in your country before shipment. Under-declaration is the buyer's legal risk, not a saving.</p>

    <h2>7. Verify on receipt</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Batch number on vial matches COA and invoice</li>
      <li>Packaging intact, cold chain maintained</li>
      <li>Appearance consistent with the COA</li>
      <li>Retain samples for your own records</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What goes wrong most often</h2>
    <p>Not product quality — documentation mismatch. The vial says one batch, the COA says another, and the buyer cannot prove what was shipped. Build the paper trail before you build the order.</p>

